<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>The scores in a quiz have mean <m>{48.1}</m> and standard deviation <m>{6.4}</m>. To put them on a new scale, every score is multiplied by <m>{2}</m> and then <m>{20}</m> is added.</p><p>Give exact values.</p><p>(a) Find the new mean.</p><div>New mean <m>=</m> <fillin characters="7" name="ans1"/>  </div><p>(b) Find the new standard deviation.</p><div>New standard deviation <m>=</m> <fillin characters="7" name="ans2"/>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>When every value <m>x</m> becomes <m>ax + b</m> (with <m>a</m> positive), the mean becomes <m>a\bar{x} + b</m> and the standard deviation becomes <m>a\sigma</m>. Multiplying by <m>a</m> stretches every distance from the mean by <m>a</m>; adding <m>b</m> moves every value, and the mean, by <m>b</m> but leaves the spread unchanged.</p><p>(a) New mean <m>= {2} \times {48.1} + {20} = {116.2}</m>.</p><p>(b) New standard deviation <m>= {2} \times {6.4} = {12.8}</m>. Adding <m>{20}</m> does not change it.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{581}{5}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{64}{5}</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>